WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.630
Bill C-22, the Lawful Access Act of 2026.

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:08.590
Introduced March 2026 by Minister Anandasangaree.

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:16.920
This is the third surveillance bill from the Carney government, alongside C-63 for online speech control and C-8 for cyber security.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:23.659
Together, these three bills create an integrated state surveillance architecture: C-63 controls what you say.

00:00:23.659 --> 00:00:26.659
C-8 mandates what companies report.

00:00:26.659 --> 00:00:31.295
C-22 enables what the government can intercept from telecommunications.

00:00:31.295 --> 00:00:33.943
The three-bill surveillance architecture.

00:00:33.943 --> 00:00:37.636
One minister, three bills, one integrated system.

00:00:37.636 --> 00:00:44.374
Bill C-63 creates a Digital Safety Commission with twenty-five million dollar penalties for online content.

00:00:44.374 --> 00:00:48.785
Bill C-8 mandates incident reporting from critical infrastructure.

00:00:48.785 --> 00:00:53.944
Bill C-22 creates new lawful intercept powers for telecommunications data.

00:00:53.944 --> 00:00:57.999
All three bypass traditional court oversight in different ways.

00:00:57.999 --> 00:01:00.624
What the Lawful Access Act does.

00:01:00.624 --> 00:01:05.534
The bill modernizes telecommunications intercept capabilities for law enforcement.

00:01:05.534 --> 00:01:12.715
It creates new production orders compelling service providers to hand over subscriber data, traffic data, and content data.

00:01:12.715 --> 00:01:21.454
It imposes data retention obligations — requiring telecoms to preserve communications data when served with a preservation demand, before any warrant is sought.

00:01:21.454 --> 00:01:31.022
This is the third generation of lawful access legislation after C-30 was withdrawn in 2013 and C-13 passed in 2014.

00:01:31.022 --> 00:01:32.488
The pattern.

00:01:32.488 --> 00:01:37.068
Three bills from the same government create a comprehensive surveillance architecture.

00:01:37.068 --> 00:01:45.727
This follows the documented institutional pattern: the Emergencies Act demonstrated the government will use extraordinary powers and face no consequences.

00:01:45.727 --> 00:01:50.717
Bill C-70 shows it will create accountability frameworks but not enforce them.

00:01:50.717 --> 00:02:00.215
Now Bills C-63, C-8, and C-22 expand the enforcement powers that will go unenforced against political actors but fully enforced against citizens.
